__notes__.note.singularity.setting-step-3-apply-ru:
Rule 1 adjoins one hyperbolic plane, minimally and uniquely \cite[Thms.~4.10, 4.13]{ButlerT1}, with minimality holding even against arbitrary unital embeddings \cite[Thm.~4.19]{ButlerT1}: $\Cl(1,3) \hookrightarrow \Cl(2,4)$, with adjoined null pair $(v, v^*)$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
v^2 = (v^*)^2 = 0, \qquad v v^* + v^* v = 1 . \tag{CAR}
\end{equation}
At the geometric level, the localisation theorem of the sheaf paper \cite[Thm.~4.4, Cor.~4.5]{ButlerT4}, extended to exact Schwarzschild by its composite \cite[Thms 8.7, 8.12]{ButlerT4}, performs this ascent fibrewise over the degenerate locus and only there: the completed pair exists exactly where the division fails. Those theorems are stated for tame interior walls, whose hypotheses exact Schwarzschild does not satisfy (the singular locus is a worldline of codimension three, and the degeneration is anisotropic); the import at the black hole is nevertheless not by analogy, because the Schwarzschild composite of \cite[\S 8]{ButlerT4} extends the localisation to this very case: after the real blow-up of the worldline and in the forced weighted coframe module, the degeneration is boundary-tame of corank one with the Killing-time covector as its radical \cite[Thm.~8.7]{ButlerT4}, the completion is performed with the ladder pair supported exactly on the blown-up singularity $E \cong \R \times S^2$, and no shadow wall forms \cite[Thm.~8.12]{ButlerT4}. The ascent this paper runs is therefore theorem-backed at the black hole itself. This is the algorithm's Step 3 \cite[\S 8.1, Step 3]{ButlerT1}.

__notes__.note.transport.setting-quadratic-spaces-:
\textbf{Scope of hypotheses.} Throughout, $(\mathbb{V}, Q)$ is nondegenerate; nothing else is assumed globally, and each theorem carries its own hypotheses. The mixed-signature and dimension conditions $p, q \geq 1$ and $n \geq 3$ are local to the no-go theorem (Theorem \claimref{transport.no-go-level-elements}), which needs them: they are exactly what guarantees that null vectors exist and that the null rotations shearing their candidate partners exist. Remarks \claimref{note.transport.obstruction-parabolic} and \claimref{note.transport.hyperbolic-atom} delimit the boundary: definite signatures pose no element-level question at all, and $n = 2$ is the sharp exception where the question is posed and answered affirmatively. The completion theorems, namely totality (Theorem \claimref{transport.totality}), minimality (Theorem \claimref{transport.minimality}) and the normal form (Proposition \claimref{transport.swap-rescale-normal-form}), hold for \emph{every} nondegenerate signature, definite included; the section “The Euclidean plane: inversion in a circle on the Riemann sphere” exercises them over the base of signature $(2,0)$.

The two canonical involutions are written as in the primer \cite[Section 5]{ButlerT0}: the \emph{grade involution} $x \mapsto \hat{x}$ (the automorphism with $\hat{v} = -v$ on vectors) and the \emph{reversion} $x \mapsto \tilde{x}$ (the anti-automorphism fixing vectors). One consequence is used repeatedly enough to display, the commutation rule \cite[Lemma 9.1]{ButlerT0}: if $x$ lies in the subalgebra generated by vectors orthogonal to a vector $v$, then moving $v$ past $x$ introduces exactly a grade involution,
\begin{equation}
v\,x = \hat{x}\,v, \qquad x\,v = v\,\hat{x}.
\end{equation}

__notes__.note.transport.setting-symmetry-action-e:
Versors, the reflection formula, and the groups $\Pin(p,q)$, $\Spin(p,q)$, $\Spin^0(p,q)$ and the rotors are as in the primer \cite[Section 6]{ButlerT0}. What the statements below need is the action: a versor $g$ (a product of invertible vectors) acts on vectors by the twisted adjoint $\Ad_g(x) = \hat{g}\, x\, g^{-1}$, realising the full orthogonal group $O(p,q)$, and the action extends to the whole algebra as
\begin{equation}
\tau_g(x) \;=\;
\begin{cases}
\,g\, x\, g^{-1} & g \text{ even},\\[2pt]
\,g\, \hat{x}\, g^{-1} & g \text{ odd},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
an algebra automorphism restricting on vectors to $\Ad_g$ \cite[Lemma 9.2]{ButlerT0}. For even elements, which are all that our proofs use, $\tau_g$ is plain conjugation.

obj.transport.obstruction-types.formal:
Let $A = \Cl(p,q)$, nondegenerate. A zero-divisor obstruction of $A$ is classified as:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Type N (isotropic vector).} A null vector $v \in \mathbb{V}$, $v \neq 0$, $v^2 = Q(v) = 0$, together with the annihilating idempotent $wv$ it acquires through a hyperbolic partner (Example \claimref{note.transport.hyperbolic-partner-supplies-annihilating-idempotent}). Geometrically: an isotropic line, a direction without a metric partner.
\item \textbf{Type P (non-central idempotent).} An idempotent $e = e^2$ with $e \notin \{0, 1\}$ and $e$ not central. Prototype: $e = \tfrac12(1 + u)$ with $u^2 = 1$, $u \neq \pm 1$. Each such $e$ is a zero divisor via $e(1 - e) = 0$.
\item \textbf{Type C (central idempotent).} An idempotent $e \notin \{0,1\}$ lying in the centre of $A$. The two-sided ideals $eA$ and $(1-e)A$ then annihilate one another and $A$ splits as their direct sum.
\end{itemize}

transport.cohomological-form-no-go.formal:
With trivial coefficients, $H^1(N_v;\, U) = \mathrm{Hom}(N_v, U)$, one-coboundaries vanishing; and the class of the pseudo-inverse torsor is the tautological homomorphism $\theta$, which is nonzero whenever $U \neq 0$. An $N_v$-invariant pseudo-inverse of $v$ exists, in the given extension, if and only if this class vanishes, if and only if $U = 0$, i.e.\ $n = 2$: the hyperbolic atom of Remark \claimref{note.transport.hyperbolic-atom} is recovered as the exact vanishing locus of the class. Three consequences:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Enlargement-independence, cohomologically.} The group $N_v$, the coefficient module $U$, and the class $\theta$ are all constructed inside $\Cl(\mathbb{V})$ and act identically in every extension; the obstruction is pulled back unchanged along every ascent, which is the cohomological restatement of the quantification over all ascents in Theorem \claimref{transport.no-go-level-elements}.
\item \emph{Levi equivariance.} By Lemma \claimref{transport.group-null-rotations}, $\theta$ intertwines the conjugation action of $\Spin(U)$ on $N_v$ with its vector action on $U$: the class is equivariant for the Levi factor of the stabiliser.
\item \emph{The full stabiliser.} A pseudo-inverse selection equivariant under any subgroup of the stabiliser of $v$ containing $N_v$, in particular under the full parabolic stabiliser of the null direction, would restrict to an $N_v$-fixed point; nonvanishing of $\theta$ on the radical therefore obstructs every enlargement of the symmetry group at once.
\end{enumerate}
